WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.236
Teams validate validation logic through
review, meaningful tests,

2
00:00:04.316 --> 00:00:06.184
and tool-supported diagnostics.

3
00:00:06.264 --> 00:00:09.163
The first step is to turn assumptions into
test cases.

4
00:00:09.243 --> 00:00:13.301
Boundary tests should cover empty input,
minimum values, maximum values,

5
00:00:13.381 --> 00:00:17.311
one-beyond limits, missing terminators,
short records, long records,

6
00:00:17.391 --> 00:00:19.130
and values near numeric limits.

7
00:00:19.210 --> 00:00:21.916
Malformed input tests exercise rejection
paths.

8
00:00:21.996 --> 00:00:26.376
They should include missing fields,
duplicate fields, unexpected delimiters,

9
00:00:26.456 --> 00:00:30.579
invalid characters, embedded null bytes
where relevant, wrong encodings,

10
00:00:30.659 --> 00:00:33.106
leftover numeric text, incomplete records,

11
00:00:33.186 --> 00:00:35.763
and inconsistent cross-field
relationships.

12
00:00:35.843 --> 00:00:39.322
Rejection behavior is part of the product,
not an afterthought.

13
00:00:39.402 --> 00:00:43.138
Regression tests should be added when
validation defects are fixed.

14
00:00:43.218 --> 00:00:47.469
A corrected validation rule is easier to
preserve when a test describes the old

15
00:00:47.549 --> 00:00:49.675
assumption and the intended behavior.

16
00:00:49.755 --> 00:00:54.200
This is especially useful for parsers and
configuration formats that evolve over

17
00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:54.602
time.

18
00:00:54.682 --> 00:00:56.743
Compiler warnings, static analysis,

19
00:00:56.823 --> 00:00:59.658
and sanitizers can help reveal missed
assumptions.

20
00:00:59.738 --> 00:01:04.376
Warnings may point to suspicious
conversions, ignored results, or bounds concerns.

21
00:01:04.456 --> 00:01:07.741
Static analysis can show paths where
validation is skipped.

22
00:01:07.821 --> 00:01:12.459
Runtime diagnostics can expose memory
mistakes when tests exercise relevant cases.

23
00:01:12.539 --> 00:01:14.600
Code review ties the pieces together.

24
00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:18.868
Reviewers should ask where input comes
from, what trust boundary it crossed,

25
00:01:18.948 --> 00:01:23.006
what contract defines valid data, whether
validation happens before use,

26
00:01:23.086 --> 00:01:27.659
what happens on failure, and whether tests
cover both boundary and malformed cases.

27
00:01:27.739 --> 00:01:31.540
Tools help, but the quality of validation
comes from clear contracts.
